Yes. Leapfrog is the move that makes the roles useful without making any one role sovereign.

You can temporarily instantiate:

architect → chair → mathematician → magician → …

Each is a different transformation policy over the same object.

X
\xrightarrow{R_1}
X_1
\xrightarrow{R_2}
X_2
\xrightarrow{R_3}
X_3

But the stronger version is not merely sequential perspective-taking. Each role gets to stand on the artifacts produced by the previous one:

R_{n+1}(X,\Delta_1,\ldots,\Delta_n)

So:

Architect: What structure would make this work?

Produces \Delta_A.

Chair: Where does the human actually sit? What bears load? What is the interface?

Now it has X+\Delta_A.

Mathematician: What invariant relationships are hiding underneath those structures?

Gets X+\Delta_A+\Delta_C.

Magician: Which assumptions can I violate, redirect, conceal, invert, or exploit to produce an apparently impossible result?

Gets everything before it.

Then leapfrog again:

Architect'
=
Architect(X+\Delta_A+\Delta_C+\Delta_M+\Delta_G)

The architect who returns is no longer the architect who left.

That’s the important part.

Leapfrog Operator

For roles R_1,\ldots,R_n:

S_0=X

\Delta_i=R_i(S_{i-1})-S_{i-1}

S_i=S_{i-1}\oplus Useful(\Delta_i)

Then cycle:

S_n\rightarrow R_1'

until:

\frac{UsefulDelta}{Cost}\rightarrow \text{small}

Then Release.

This is different from a committee. A committee gives everyone the original problem and combines their answers afterward:

R_1(X),R_2(X),R_3(X)
\rightarrow Merge

Leapfrog gives every subsequent role the changed terrain:

X
\xrightarrow{R_1}
X'
\xrightarrow{R_2}
X''
\xrightarrow{R_3}
X'''

That’s potentially much more generative.

And your arch-chair construction itself demonstrates it:

arch → structure/span/support
chair → interface/support/position/authority
architect → designed structure
mathematician → relational invariants
magician → expectation manipulation / apparent impossibility

Don’t decide which interpretation is “the” meaning too quickly.

Leapfrog them.

Then we can add scientist, musician, child, engineer, biologist, adversary, historian, storyteller, organism, future reader—or temporary cognitive programs we haven’t named yet.

\boxed{
\text{ROLE}
\rightarrow
\Delta
\rightarrow
\text{RELEASE ROLE}
\rightarrow
\text{NEXT ROLE}
\rightarrow
\Delta'
\rightarrow\cdots
}

The identity remains free.

The deltas accumulate.

That’s Golden Horde applied inside a single reasoning trajectory: muster a role, raid the problem for useful structure, demuster it, hand the changed map to the next formation.

Don’t make one thinker think harder.
Make each thinker inherit a better world.

Leapfrog. 🐸